paragraphs.en:
I cannot bear to climb the river pavilion on the Paesu and chant;
the sobbing river flows with a sound that breaks the heart.
The old country has already lost its dragon-and-tiger spirit,
and the ruined city still wears the shape of a phoenix.
On the white-lit sandbar wild geese lose their way home;
in the courtyard, as the mist lifts, dew-wet fireflies dot the grass.
The scenery is bleak and human affairs have changed;
in the temple at Cold Mountain I hear the bell toll.

In the imperial palace the autumn grass is cold and desolate;
the winding stone steps are hidden by cloud, and I lose my path.
At the courtesans' halls the old foundations are choked with shepherd's purse;
on the parapet the waning moon, and crows cry in the night.
The glories of elegance have turned to dust and earth;
the silent empty city is overgrown with caltrop and thorn.
Only the river waves still sob as of old,
rolling on to the sea gate in the west.

The waters of the Paesu are bluer than indigo;
a thousand ages of rise and fall, a sorrow hard to bear.
The golden well is dry, hung with creeping fig;
the stone altar is eaten by moss, embraced by tamarisk and nan trees.
Wind and moon of a foreign land, a thousand poems;
thoughts of the old country, wine half drunk.
Under the white moon I lean on the rail and cannot sleep;
deep in the night fragrant cassia drops in drooping tangles.

The moon rises over the eastern hill, magpies fly;
deep in the night the cold dew soaks a man's clothes.
A thousand years of institutions and attire are gone;
ten thousand ages of mountains, rivers, walls and ramparts are not what they were.
The sage ruler who went to court in Heaven does not come back;
idle tales of the fallen world: in the end on whom do they rest?
The golden carriage and the unicorn steed have left no trace;
on the imperial road the grass is wild, and a monk goes home alone.

In the courtyard the grass is cold with autumn and the jade dew withers;
Blue Cloud Bridge faces White Cloud Bridge.
The soldiers of the house of Sui followed the sounding rapids;
the prince's spirit has turned into a resentful cicada.
The royal road is buried in mist, the fragrant carriages cut off;
at the traveling palace the pines lean and the evening bell sways.
I climb high and make a rhapsody, but who will share it?
The moon is white, the wind clear, and my zest has not faded.

The pavilion tonight has much bright moon;
how can clear talk bear such stirred emotion?
The colors of the trees faintly spread like blue canopies;
the rippling river trails like a white silk skirt.
Time is suddenly spent, like a flying bird;
the affairs of the world startle again and again, like waves that pass.
Who can fully grasp the feelings of this evening?
A few notes of bell and chime come out of the creeping mist.

Looking south from the old city, the Paesu divides;
the water is blue, the sand bright, a flock of geese is crying.
The unicorn chariot does not come, the dragon has already gone;
the phoenix flute once ceased, and the earth became a tomb.
The haze clears as if it would rain, and the poem is rounded off;
in the wild temple there is no one, and the wine is half-heady.
How can I bear to see the bronze camels sunk in thorns?
A thousand years of traces have turned into floating cloud.

Grass roots sob, a cold cicada weeps;
once I climb the high pavilion, thought goes far and dim.
Broken rain and lingering cloud: I grieve over the past;
fallen blossoms and flowing water: I feel the passing of time.
The waves add autumn air, the sound of the tide is strong;
the tower dips in the river's heart, the moonlight cold.
This was the land of culture in former years;
now the ruined city's sparse trees vex a man's heart.

Before Mt. Geumsu, brocade heaped on brocade;
river maples reflect against the corner of the ancient city.
Ding-dong: where does the bitter autumn pounding of the washing-block come from?
One cry of "aenae" and a fishing boat turns back.
Old trees lean on a crag, wound with creeping fig;
a broken stele lies across the grass, drawing the moss.
Leaning on the rail, speechless, I grieve over the past;
moonlight and wave-sound are all sorrow together.

A few sparse stars dot the Jade Capital;
the Silver River is clear and shallow, the moon distinct.
Only now I know that all good affairs are empty affairs;
it is hard to foretell meeting in another life as in this.
A cup of fine wine is good for getting drunk;
three feet of worldly dust: do not let it entangle the heart.
Heroes through ten thousand ages turn to dust and earth;
in the world only an empty name remains after death.

How late is the night? The night is turning toward its end;
on the parapet the waning moon is round and round.
Now you and I are of course sundered by two kinds of dust;
meeting me, you instead gain a joy of a thousand days.
On the river the jade towers: the people are about to scatter;
before the steps the jade trees: the dew has just spread.
If you would know where we shall meet again after this,
it will be when the peaches ripen on Mt. Peng and the blue sea runs dry.

Moon-white, the river pavilion by night;
in the long sky the jade dew flows.
Its pure light dips into the Milky Way;
its vast air blankets the parasol and catalpa trees.
Shining pale over three thousand worlds,
radiant over the twelve towers.
Not a wisp of cloud, not half a speck;
a light breeze wipes my two eyes.
It glitters, following the flowing water;
faintly it sends off the departing boat.
It can peer into the gaps of a thatched door;
it shines most on the isle of reed blossom.
It seems like hearing the Rainbow-Skirt music played,
like watching the jade axe do its trimming.
Oyster pearls brood in the shell palace;
the rhinoceros halo overturns Jambudvipa.
I wish to enjoy it with Zhiwei,
and ever follow Gongyuan in his travels.
Its cold points startle the magpie of Wei;
its shadow shoots at the panting buffalo of Wu.
Dimly the blue hills' ramparts,
round and round the blue sea's corner.
With you I open the key-lock,
in high spirits we climb to the curtain hook.
It is the day Master Li stopped his cup,
the autumn Wu cut the cassia.
The white screen gleams brilliantly;
the silk tent is finely carved.
The precious mirror is polished and newly hung;
the eternal wheel is driven on and does not stay.
How solemn the golden waves!
The silver water-clock drips on and on.
Draw the sword and slay the demon toad;
spread the net and snare the hare.
On the sky road the rain has newly cleared;
on the stone path the thin mist withdraws.
The balustrade presses on a thousand trees,
the steps look down on a ten-thousand-fathom pool.
In the passes and rivers who has lost the road?
In my homeland, luckily, I meet a companion.
Peach and plum trade gifts and returns;
jars and cups may be offered and answered.
For fine poems we vie as the candle is notched;
of fine wine there is more than enough, and the tally-sticks grow.
In the brazier the black silver flakes crackle;
in the kettle the crab-eye bubbles turn.
Dragon spittle incense flies from the sleeping-duck burner;
jewelled liquor fills the knobbed bowl.
A crane cries and the lone pine is startled;
a cicada chirps and all four walls are sad.
On the barbarian stool, an earnest, lean talk;
on the Jin shore, Xie's distant journey.
It is as if the ruined city were still there,
the grass and trees dense and rustling.
The green maples sway in the clear dew;
the yellow reeds are cold and whistling.
In the immortal's mirror heaven and earth are wide;
amid the dust the sexagenary years close in.
In the old palace the millet ears hang;
in the wild shrine the catalpa and mulberry droop.
Fragrance and stench remain on a broken stele;
rise and fall are asked of the drifting gulls.
The moon (Xian'e) is ever waxing and waning,
and the heaped clods are but a few mayflies.
The traveling palace has become a monk's cell;
the former king is buried on Tiger Hill.
Fireflies' phosphor is small beyond the curtain;
ghost fires glow by the forest.
Lamenting the past, I shed many tears;
grieving for the present, I buy my own sorrow.
Of Dangun there remains Mongmyeok;
of Gija's town there is only Guru.
In the cave are the tracks of the unicorn;
on the plain one meets the arrows of Sushen.
The fragrance of orchids returns to the Purple Prefecture;
the Weaver Maid drives her green dragon.
The man of letters lays down his flowery brush;
the immortal lady stops her watchtower beacons.
The song ends, the people are about to scatter;
the wind is still, and the sound of the oars is soft.
